    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/sukaphaa"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sukaphaa.jpg" alt="Sukaphaa" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/sukaphaa">Sukaphaa</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sukapha.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">govt of assam stamp, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Ahom kingdom was founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sukaphaa">Sukaphaa</a>, a Tai prince from the Shan country of Mong Mao in what is now northern Myanmar and south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. According to the Ahom chronicles he left his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bantustan">homeland</a> with a band of followers, crossed the Patkai hills and reached the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmaputra">Brahmaputra</a> valley in 1228. For more than two decades the newcomers moved between temporary settlements before Sukaphaa fixed his seat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charaideo">Charaideo</a>, traditionally around 1253. The local <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moran-people">Moran</a> and Borahi peoples were absorbed through conquest, intermarriage and the grant of land, a pattern of assimilation that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahom-people">Ahoms</a> repeated as they grew. The early kingdom was small, confined to the land between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dikhow-river">Dikhou</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burhi-dihing-river">Dihing</a> rivers, and its neighbours were stronger: the Chutia kingdom to the north-east and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dimasa-kingdom">Kachari kingdom</a> to the south-west. Over the following three centuries Sukaphaa's successors pushed westwards along the river, opening new land to wet-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> cultivation, which the Tai settlers brought with them and which allowed far denser populations than the shifting agriculture of the hills. The decisive expansion came under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suhungmung">Suhungmung</a> (r. 1497–1539), who annexed the Chutia kingdom in 1523, drove the Kacharis from their capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dimapur">Dimapur</a> and turned a regional chiefdom into the dominant power of the Brahmaputra valley.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/charaideo"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/charaideo.jpg" alt="Charaideo" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/charaideo">Charaideo</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Charadeo_Maidam_mausoleum.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Zorodocknife, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Ahom economy rested on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wet-rice-agriculture">wet-rice agriculture</a> in the fertile floodplain of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmaputra">Brahmaputra</a>, supported by embankments, roads and irrigation channels built by state labour. Its foundation was the paik system: every free adult man was registered as a paik and grouped with others in a unit called a <em>got</em>, whose members took turns serving the state for part of the year as soldiers, builders or workers while the others farmed their plots. In return each paik received a grant of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> land. Under King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pratap-singha">Pratap Singha</a> (r. 1603–1641) the official <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/momai-tamuli-borbarua">Momai Tamuli Borbarua</a> reorganised the paiks into professional <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guilds</a> called <em>khels</em>, which supplied the state with boatmen, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archery">archers</a>, potters, goldwashers and other specialists. The economy used little coinage until the mid-seventeenth century, when octagonal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> and gold coins were struck in the king's name. Trade with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a>, Bhutan, the hill peoples and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> brought salt, horses and cloth, though the kings kept it under tight control. Society was gradually transformed as the Tai elite intermarried with Assamese-speaking communities. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hinduism</a> spread at court from the sixteenth century, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ekasarana-dharma">Neo-Vaishnavite</a> monasteries (<em>satras</em>) inspired by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srimanta-sankardev">Srimanta Sankardev</a> won wide popular followings. Ahom priests, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deodhai">Deodhais</a> and Bailungs, kept older Tai rites alive, including the burial of royalty in mounds called <em>maidams</em> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charaideo">Charaideo</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-saraighat"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-saraighat.jpg" alt="Battle of Saraighat" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-saraighat">Battle of Saraighat</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Battle_of_saraighat.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">User:Chaipau, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The paik system made the Ahom kingdom a society organised for war: in an emergency a large share of the male population could be mobilised quickly, and the same officers who supervised labour commanded troops. Armies fought mainly as infantry armed with bows, swords, spears and, from the sixteenth century, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matchlock">matchlocks</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/field-artillery">cannon</a>, and they made skilful use of earthen ramparts, stockades and the dense forests and rivers of the valley. A fleet of war boats on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmaputra">Brahmaputra</a> was equally important. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suhungmung">Suhungmung</a> defeated an invasion from the Bengal Sultanate in the early 1530s. From 1615 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahom-people">Ahoms</a> fought a long series of wars with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire</a> over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamrup">Kamrup</a> in the lower valley; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-asurar-ali">Treaty of Asurar Ali</a> in 1639 fixed a frontier between them. In 1662 the Mughal governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mir-jumla-ii">Mir Jumla</a>, invaded, occupied the capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garhgaon">Garhgaon</a> and forced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jayadhwaj-singha">Jayadhwaj Singha</a> to accept the harsh <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-ghilajharighat">Treaty of Ghilajharighat</a> in 1663, but disease and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monsoon">monsoon</a> floods ruined the Mughal army. The Ahoms retook <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guwahati">Guwahati</a> in 1667, and in 1671 the general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lachit-borphukan">Lachit Borphukan</a> defeated a Mughal force under Raja <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ram-singh-i">Ram Singh I of Amber</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-saraighat">Battle of Saraighat</a>, fought largely on the river. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gadadhar-singha">Gadadhar Singha</a> finally expelled the Mughals from Guwahati in 1682, fixing the western frontier at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manas-river">Manas River</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/gadadhar-singha"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gadadhar-singha.jpg" alt="Gadadhar Singha" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/gadadhar-singha">Gadadhar Singha</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Swargadeo_Gadadhar_Singha.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Kabiraj chakravarthy, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The king, styled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swargadeo">Swargadeo</a>, "lord of heaven", had to be a descendant of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sukaphaa">Sukaphaa</a> without physical blemish, and he ruled with a council of great ministers. The oldest offices were the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burhagohain">Burhagohain</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borgohain">Borgohain</a>, hereditary in particular Ahom noble families, to which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suhungmung">Suhungmung</a> added the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borpatrogohain">Borpatrogohain</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pratap-singha">Pratap Singha</a> created two further offices: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borbarua">Borbarua</a>, who handled revenue and justice in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-assam">Upper Assam</a>, and the Borphukan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroy-of-india">viceroy</a> of the lower valley with his seat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guwahati">Guwahati</a> after its recovery. Below them a hierarchy of officers commanded fixed numbers of paiks: the Bora 20, the Saikia 100 and the Hazarika 1,000, with the Rajkhowa and the Phukan above them. This structure combined civil administration, labour service and military command in one chain. Regular <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">censuses</a> of the paiks were made, and the state kept chronicles called <em>buranjis</em>, written first in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahom-people">Tai Ahom</a> language and later in Assamese, which record campaigns, embassies and court affairs year by year. The court moved from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charaideo">Charaideo</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garhgaon">Garhgaon</a> in the sixteenth century and later to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rangpur-assam">Rangpur</a>, where kings built the brick palaces of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/talatal-ghar">Talatal Ghar</a> and Kareng Ghar and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rang-ghar">Rang Ghar</a> pavilion. Succession disputes were frequent, and in the 1670s and 1680s ministers made and unmade several kings before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gadadhar-singha">Gadadhar Singha</a> restored royal authority.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/sivadol"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sivadol.jpg" alt="Sivadol" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/sivadol">Sivadol</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Siva_Dol.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Aniruddha Buragohain, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Ahom kingdom is the central state in the history of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assam">Assam</a>, and its six centuries of rule shaped the region's identity more than any other polity. Its resistance to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire</a> was unusual on the subcontinent: while most of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> came under Mughal control, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahom-people">Ahoms</a> held the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmaputra">Brahmaputra</a> valley, and the victory at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-saraighat">Saraighat</a> remains a point of pride in modern Assam, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lachit-divas">Lachit Divas</a> is celebrated each November. The kingdom also illustrates how a small group of Tai migrants could build a lasting state by assimilating local peoples; over time the Ahoms adopted Assamese, which became the language of administration and literature, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> religious practice, while preserving Tai rituals, titles and the royal burial mounds. The <em>buranjis</em> form one of the richest bodies of historical writing in pre-colonial India and are a main source for the region's history. Ahom kings patronised temples and tanks around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sivasagar">Sivasagar</a>, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sivadol">Sivadol</a>, and the moidams at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charaideo">Charaideo</a> were inscribed as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 2024. Ahom place names, institutions and memories of the paik system remained part of Assamese life long after 1826, and the Tai Ahom community continues to claim descent from the founders of the kingdom.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/rudra-singha"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rudra-singha.jpg" alt="Rudra Singha" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/rudra-singha">Rudra Singha</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rudra_Singha_Jayata_in_court.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown authorUnknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The kingdom reached its height under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rudra-singha">Rudra Singha</a> (r. 1696–1714) and his successors, but strains grew within it during the eighteenth century. Royal patronage of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaktism">Shaktism</a> alienated many Vaishnavite <em>satras</em>, and the persecution of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mayamara-satra">Moamaria</a> monastery and its largely lower-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a> and tribal followers set off the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moamoria-rebellion">Moamoria rebellion</a> in 1769. In two great waves the rebels seized the capital, and King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaurinath-singha">Gaurinath Singha</a> fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guwahati">Guwahati</a>; at his request the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">British East India Company</a> sent Captain Thomas Welsh with a small force in 1792, which restored him before being recalled in 1794. The paik system was disrupted, large areas were depopulated and the minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/purnananda-burhagohain">Purnananda Burhagohain</a> governed with difficulty. Court factionalism then opened the door to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a>: the dismissed Borphukan Badan Chandra sought help from the Konbaung kings, and Burmese armies entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assam">Assam</a> in 1817, returning in 1819 and 1821, when they made the valley a province of Burma. Their occupation was remembered for its violence and caused heavy loss of population. The British, at war with Burma from 1824, expelled the Burmese army, and by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-yandabo">Treaty of Yandabo</a> in 1826 Burma renounced its claims to Assam. The Company briefly installed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/purandar-singha">Purandar Singha</a> as a tributary ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-assam">Upper Assam</a> in 1833 but annexed his territory in 1838, ending Ahom rule.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Ahom kingdom ruled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmaputra">Brahmaputra</a> valley for six centuries, turning a band of Tai migrants into the dominant power of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assam">Assam</a> through the paik labour system, resisting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-saraighat">Saraighat</a> and leaving the chronicles, monuments and identity on which modern Assam was built.</em></p>

